# No legacy VSE `.sequences`: use `.strips`

Blender 5.0 renamed the sequencer's strip collections. `SequenceEditor.sequences`
and `SequenceEditor.sequences_all` were removed; `.strips` and `.strips_all`
replace them. 4.5 LTS already has both names pointing at the same strips, so
`.strips` is correct on every supported version and needs no `hasattr` or
version check.

Measured with `hasattr(scene.sequence_editor_create(), name)`:

| Attribute | 4.5.14 LTS | 5.2.1 LTS |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `sequences` | `True` | `False` |
| `sequences_all` | `True` | `False` |
| `strips` | `True` | `True` |
| `strips_all` | `True` | `True` |

## What this rule flags

- `sequence_editor.sequences` or `se.sequences` on any `SequenceEditor`,
  including `.sequences.new_effect(...)`, `.sequences.new_movie(...)` and
  iteration
- `sequence_editor.sequences_all`
- An `se.strips or se.sequences` fallback: an empty collection is falsy, so
  on an empty 5.x timeline it reaches `.sequences` and raises

## Wrong

```python
import bpy

se = bpy.context.scene.sequence_editor_create()
strip = se.sequences.new_effect(
    name="Title", type="COLOR", channel=1, frame_start=1, frame_end=48,
)
for s in se.sequences_all:
    print(s.name)
```

On 5.x the second line raises
`AttributeError: 'SequenceEditor' object has no attribute 'sequences'`.

## Right

```python
import bpy

se = bpy.context.scene.sequence_editor_create()
coll = se.strips  # present on 4.5 LTS and 5.x
for s in se.strips_all:
    print(s.name)
```

`new_effect` itself also changed its end keyword between 4.5 and 5.x
(`frame_end` vs `length`); that split does need a version branch, shown in
the vse-python skill.

